+### OpenJ9 for iOS
+
+The OpenJ9 iOS target is cross-compiled on an Apple Silicon Mac with Xcode.
+Use `--openjdk-target=aarch64-apple-ios` and
+`--with-sdk-name=iphoneos` for a device, or
+`--with-sdk-name=iphonesimulator` for the arm64 simulator. The option
+`--with-ios-version-min=15.0` selects the deployment minimum (15.0 by default).
+Pass a matching macOS Semeru JDK 21 as both `--with-boot-jdk` and
+`--with-build-jdk`, together with `--with-noncompressedrefs --disable-ddr
+--without-macosx-codesign`.
+
+The target retains the macOS Java source selection and module platform, but
+uses the iOS SDK and compiler target triple. Build tools use the macOS SDK.
+The OpenJ9 CMake build uses the `ios_aarch64` interpreter-only profile; its
+build-time generators are retargeted and ad-hoc signed by
+`closed/autoconf/ios-host-exec.sh` before running on the host.
+
+The image excludes desktop-dependent modules, macOS keychain and native
+Kerberos-cache libraries, and executable launchers. Process creation and
+the JDWP `launch=` option report an unsupported operation. Use `make jdk-image`
+to produce the target jmods for a host-side `jlink` invocation. The resulting
+libraries and Java home must be packaged in a signed iOS application; this
+build does not itself produce a deployable application. Interpreter-only
+execution does not guarantee App Store approval: public API use, signing,
+entitlements and App Review requirements apply separately.
+
